---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Password Toggle Rollout">
  <h1>Password Toggle Rollout</h1>
  <p>
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_SIGNUP_ENABLED</code> and
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_RESET_ENABLED</code> are deployment-scoped
    boot-time flags. Identity reads them once at process start. There is
    no hot reload path, no admin RPC to flip them, and no repo-backed
    feature-flag store for them.
  </p>

  <h2>Decision</h2>
  <p>
    Runtime reload is intentionally not implemented. To change either
    toggle in a multi-replica deployment, update the environment and
    perform a rolling restart.
  </p>
  <p>
    Until the rollout finishes, old replicas continue serving the old
    behavior. Treat this as a normal config rollout, not a live switch.
  </p>

  <h2>When to use each toggle</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false</code></td>
      <td>Self-serve <code>PasswordSignup</code> is rejected. Invitation-driven account creation still works.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_RESET_ENABLED=false</code></td>
      <td><code>RequestPasswordReset</code> remains enumeration-safe but becomes a no-op. Admin reset paths still work.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Runbook</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Update the deployment manifest, secret, or environment source with the new toggle values.</li>
    <li>Roll the deployment so every replica restarts with the same config.</li>
    <li>Wait for all old replicas to drain before treating the new behavior as globally active.</li>
    <li>Verify the new behavior with direct RPC checks.</li>
    <li>Watch logs and auth-error rate during the rollout window.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Verification</h2>
  <p>
    After the last old replica is gone, verify the externally visible
    behavior.
  </p>
  <Code code={`# Expect FailedPrecondition once signup is disabled.
curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{
    "email": "rollout-check@example.com",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
  }'

# RequestPasswordReset still returns success when reset is disabled,
# but should not create a token or send recovery email.
curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/RequestPasswordReset \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email": "rollout-check@example.com"}'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Operational notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not mix toggle changes with JWT key rotation or TOTP key changes in the same rollout unless you have to. Keep auth-behavior changes easy to isolate.</li>
    <li>If you need an instant global cutoff for self-serve signup or reset, use the load balancer or deployment layer to drain all old replicas quickly. Identity itself does not provide a stronger runtime control today.</li>
    <li>For repeatable production checks, automate one post-rollout probe for signup rejection and one for reset no-op behavior.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
